 # Subscription quota contract (macOS and Windows)
 
-Subscription quota is an account-level observation, distinct from token usage and spend derived from session logs. Local providers read the local application's session; remote providers read the corresponding session on an enabled SSH host and query the supplier there. Credentials never cross the SSH quota protocol, enter logs or get written back. The endpoints and fields below are observed provider behavior, not guarantees of stable public APIs.
+Subscription quota is an account-level observation, distinct from token usage and spend derived from session logs. Local providers read credentials on the desktop; remote providers read credentials on an enabled SSH host and query the supplier there. Borrowed tool credentials remain read-only. Quota requests and observations never contain secrets. The sole credential-transfer operation is an explicit user action to save a newly entered Kimi Code API key from desktop Settings to the selected remote host over its existing encrypted SSH stdin channel; the remote never returns that key. Credentials never enter logs. The endpoints and fields below are observed provider behavior, not guarantees of stable public APIs.
 
 On macOS and Windows, the local Codex provider reads `auth.json` from a non-empty `CODEX_HOME` directory when set. Otherwise, it reads `~/.codex/auth.json` on macOS or `%USERPROFILE%\.codex\auth.json` on Windows. The Linux collector follows the same `CODEX_HOME` / `~/.codex/auth.json` convention on the remote host. Files are read-only; credentials are used only for requests made on their owning host. Local and remote sources follow the same snapshot, endpoint, status, freshness and backoff semantics below.
 
 ## Remote subscription sources
 
-Windows and macOS offer Codex and OpenCode Go for each enabled SSH host in the existing Subscription source selector. The local IDs remain `codex` and `opencode-go`; remote selections use `remote:<host UUID>:<provider ID>`. Host identity, not its editable label or account label, partitions sources. All sources includes enabled remote hosts and future sources; explicit selection includes only its saved IDs. Remote-only operation does not require a local account. Each selected source uses the ordinary account cards, floating quota panel and summaries, with host provenance; independent allowances are never summed.
+Windows and macOS offer Codex, OpenCode Go and Kimi Code for each enabled SSH host in the existing Subscription source selector. The local IDs are `codex`, `opencode-go` and `kimi`; remote selections use `remote:<host UUID>:<provider ID>`. Host identity, not its editable label or account label, partitions sources. All sources includes enabled remote hosts and future sources; explicit selection includes only its saved IDs. Remote-only operation does not require a local account. Each selected source uses the ordinary account cards, floating quota panel and summaries, with host provenance; independent allowances are never summed.
 
-Requests use the existing collector connection, not a separate SSH process. The desktop sends a bounded JSON line with `type: "quota"`, an opaque `requestId`, `providers` (a nonempty subset of `codex` and `opencode-go`) and `refresh`. `refresh: false` reads the current identity's cached observation without supplier HTTP. `refresh: true` requests supplier observations subject to persisted backoff and request floors. No selected, consented quota request means no supplier polling. Automatic mode follows the existing five-minute application cadence; manual requests respect provider deadlines. Hiding Settings or the floating panel does not change polling.
