 The collector responds only to the requesting subscriber with `type: "quota"`, the matching `requestId` and a `snapshots` array containing one result for every requested provider. Snapshot fields follow the contract below, with bare provider IDs, camelCase keys, UTC ISO-8601 dates, durations in seconds and nullable unknown values. Responses contain structured observations only: never credential contents, authorization headers or raw supplier payloads. The desktop maps provider IDs into host-scoped source IDs and keeps supplier branding separate from source identity.
 
 Quota work runs outside sampling and subscriber-output locks. Coalesced requests cannot block metrics, another subscriber, relay publication or widget publication. Desktop requests time out after 45 seconds; an older collector that ignores quota commands produces an honest unavailable state, not an endless refresh. Disconnects invalidate in-flight correlation, retain previous successful readings as stale with their original observation time and never invent zero allowance. Removing/disabling a host or excluding a provider removes its active source and invalidates late responses. Revoking network consent prevents new supplier requests and late results from restoring a revoked observation. Reconnection uses a new request generation.
 
-Remote credentials and account-isolated supplier caches remain on the remote machine. The desktop may persist only structured remote observations, scoped to the configured host and connection destination; restored readings are stale, preserve their original timestamps and are invalidated when that destination changes or the host is removed. Codex and Go use their own installed credential stores, endpoint selection, authentication/entitlement rules and persisted retry deadlines. A missing login is `needsAuth`, not evidence that a source does not exist. A changed or rejected identity cannot expose another account's supplier cache. Remote acquisition does not alter token-rate/history aggregation or relay authorization.
