 Go follows the pinned provider's polling semantics: successful requests do not create a hard minimum request interval, so a manual refresh may request again after the previous request completes. Automatic application polling remains five minutes; concurrent refreshes are coalesced, and persisted rate-limit/failure retry deadlines remain binding. This does not change Codex's existing successful-request floor or startup rules.
 
+## Kimi Code integration reference
+
+Kimi's original CLI OAuth path follows Codenotch's `KimiCredentials.swift`, `KimiProvider.swift` and `KimiUsage.swift` at the pinned reference commit above. It reads `credentials/kimi-code.json` under a nonempty `KIMI_CODE_HOME`, otherwise `~/.kimi-code` (the current user's home on Windows too). Require a nonempty `access_token` and a positive epoch-seconds `expires_at`. The file is read-only; Kimi Code CLI owns OAuth login and renewal. Expired credentials require CLI renewal, not an attempted supplier request or an independently refreshed token. Static Moonshot Platform API keys are not Kimi Code subscription credentials. The official [provider documentation](https://moonshotai.github.io/kimi-code/en/configuration/providers.html#oauth-and-credential-injection) confirms the managed OAuth login/renewal contract.
+
+Request `GET https://api.kimi.com/coding/v1/usages` with bearer authorization and `Accept: application/json`, with a fifteen-second timeout. Read the session and query on the source's owning host; remote responses never contain credentials or raw supplier bodies. HTTP 401/403 require authentication, 404 indicates no Kimi Code plan, and 429 establishes a persisted retry deadline (sixty seconds when no usable server guidance is supplied). Other network/service/decoding failures preserve only account-matching stale observations and their original timestamps. Successful polls impose no hard refresh floor; automatic polling remains five minutes and concurrent refreshes coalesce.
+
+Parse legacy `usage` as weekly and recognized `limits[].window` entries as five-hour or weekly, with `detail.limit`, `used` or valid `limit - remaining`, and `resetTime`. Also parse `usages.limit_5h`, `limit_7d`, `limit_month_total` and `limit_month_code`, whose finite nonnegative `used_ratio` is already a fraction and whose `reset_time` is ISO-8601. Prefer these precise ratios over legacy counts for the same window. Preserve valid siblings, known zero, values above one and unknown reset times; never infer a monthly duration. Keep total-month and code-month windows separate. `user.membership.level` supplies the optional plan; do not invent an account address. The extra-usage `boosterWallet` is not a quota window. No valid quota windows means unsupported, not zero allowance.
+
+### Kimi Code API key settings and remote provisioning
+
+Subscription Settings on both desktops offers a masked Kimi Code API key editor for the local source and each enabled SSH host. It works without a detected CLI account or an enabled quota source. Users choose China or International, enter a Code subscription key from the corresponding console, and save/verify or remove it; they never edit a remote file or run provisioning commands. The editor shows stored-key presence and region, never the saved secret, and keeps typed text out of periodic page-state snapshots. Clear input after submission, closing or source changes. Disconnected destinations cannot claim a successful save. Saving configuration does not authorize supplier requests when network consent is disabled; distinguish successful persistence from successful quota verification.
+
+After a successful save and successful quota verification, the editor dismisses automatically on both desktops. Persistence alone, disabled network consent, provider backoff, rejected keys and verification/storage/network failures keep it open with the corresponding message; metadata reads and removal do not dismiss it.
+
+This is a deliberate extension beyond the pinned Codenotch OAuth reader. [Kimi's official documentation](https://www.kimi.com/code/docs/en/#api-access) provides subscription Code keys and the regional API hosts. [CodexBar's Kimi adapter documentation](https://github.com/steipete/CodexBar/blob/main/docs/kimi.md#method-1-kimi-code-api-key-recommended) demonstrates using those keys for the same `/usages` query. China keys use `https://api.kimi.com/coding/v1/usages`, International keys use `https://api.kimi.ai/coding/v1/usages`. Never guess key region, cross-probe hosts, allow arbitrary endpoint overrides or send CLI OAuth credentials to the International host.
+
+An explicitly saved key is authoritative, has no CLI fifteen-minute expiry, and uses bearer authentication. Only actual absence permits the existing China CLI OAuth path. A rejected, corrupt or inaccessible key store must not silently change accounts by falling back to OAuth. Key authentication failures use key-specific guidance, not `/login` renewal text. Include authentication mode, region and a secret-derived digest in cache/rejection/currentness identity; key replacement, region changes and deletion invalidate in-flight old-identity results and old displayed observations. Removing a key permits subsequent CLI rediscovery, without altering the CLI session. Keep existing provider backoff and request coalescing.
+
+Local keys use system-backed secret storage (current-user DPAPI on Windows and Keychain on macOS), never ordinary settings JSON, UserDefaults, observation caches or exports. Remote keys are stored atomically as region, key and a fresh random internal revision nonce in `~/.agent-speed/quota/kimi.json`, an owned regular file with mode `0600` under owned private `0700` directories. Every set generates a new revision, including delete/recreate with an identical key, so filesystem inode reuse and timestamp granularity cannot let an old request commit. The nonce is never returned in metadata; cache/backoff identity remains bound to mode, region and key rather than the revision, so saving the same key cannot bypass a persisted supplier deadline. Reject unsafe paths, symlinks, hardlinks and insecure ownership/mode; distinguish an inaccessible or corrupt existing store from absence. No plaintext fallback is permitted for failed local secure storage.
+
+Remote management is a separate correlated control message, not a quota snapshot: `type: "kimi_credentials"`, a 1–128-character ASCII `[A-Za-z0-9_-]` `requestId`, and `operation: "get"`, `"set"` or `"delete"`. Only `set` includes `region` (`"china"` or `"international"`) and `apiKey` (1–4096 printable non-whitespace ASCII bytes). Reject unexpected fields and invalid types. Commands stay within the existing 16-KiB frame bound. Send the key only over the existing no-PTY SSH stdin channel, never command arguments or a shell command. Pending correlation state does not retain the key.
+
+Reply only to the requesting subscriber with `type: "kimi_credentials_result"`, matching `requestId` and `status: "ok"` or `"error"`. Successful replies report `configured` and `region` (null when absent); failures report a fixed `errorCode` (`"invalid_request"` or `"storage_error"`) without raw exception text. No response includes the key, fragments, a fingerprint or filesystem paths. A set/delete acknowledgment means durable storage/removal succeeded, not that supplier authentication succeeded. These operations never request supplier HTTP. Native clients separately refresh the selected source when consent permits, preserving backoff. Bound requests by timeout and host/connection generation, reject late results after disconnect or destination changes, and report older unsupported collectors honestly rather than claiming success. Same-UID collector peers can manage this same-UID store; it is not a privilege boundary against another process owned by that user.
+
